---
// ESTATE CLEANOUT — `/estate-cleanout-san-jose/`. Rebuilt following the same gold-standard
// approach as `/hoarding-cleanup-san-jose/` (docs/30-WEBSITE-MASTER-SPEC.md §20, §20A), with
// copy reconciled from docs/ASEPTACLEAN-SERVICE-LANDING-PAGE-SYSTEM.md §39 under §20A.1's order:
// landing copy → map to current route → check active/gated state → check claims, pricing and
// scope → check current copy authority → then implement. The existing `estatePage` record in
// src/data/doc27ServicePages.ts (owner-approved, route-audit-corrected copy) was mined for
// approved language — "authorized decision-maker", the donation/appraisal boundary, the FAQ
// answers — rather than discarded.
//
//   01 SERVICE HERO             photograph + overlay, left-aligned, service confirmed on sight
//   02 PROTECT WHAT MATTERS     "We do not start by clearing." + the Save List, as a document —
//                               this page's signature movement, on navy
//   03 WHO DECIDES + WHAT NEXT  decision authority, then the property's end goal — the second
//                               mechanism this page runs on
//   04 THE CLEARING PLAN        six-step field procedure, stop-and-ask, hidden conditions
//   05 CONTENTS + BOUNDARIES    disposal/donation/appraisal boundaries — what leaves, how, and
//                               who decides value
//   06 WHAT PROOF EXISTS        the honest gap, remote/out-of-area support, deadlines, fit + price
//   07 QUESTIONS                seven, the ones that decide the purchase
//   08 CLOSE                    "You do not have to decide everything today."
//
// THE SPECIALIST THESIS IS §20A.5's, unchanged: **protect what matters before the property gets
// cleared.** This is deliberately NOT Hoarding's "you stay in control" argument, even though both
// pages share a decision-authority mechanism — see the doc-precedence note below on why that
// overlap is structural, not a copy failure. Every movement reinforces protection-before-clearing:
// the hero states it, 02 is the mechanism (the Save List), 03 is who is allowed to act on it, 04
// shows it surviving contact with the property, 05 is where approved material actually goes, 06 is
// what it costs and what remote families can rely on, 07 answers it as an objection.
//
// DISTINCTION FROM HOARDING, BY DESIGN, NOT BY ACCIDENT:
//   - Hoarding's signature movement is a five-category decision REGISTER (Keep/Set aside/Donate/
//     Remove/Not sure) applied to accumulated CONTENTS. This page's signature movement is a SAVE
//     LIST — named items and named categories an authorized person defines before clearing starts
//     — applied to a property being HANDED OFF (sold, rented, returned to family, emptied). The
//     document shape, wording and purpose are both different: a save list is instructions written
//     in advance; Hoarding's register is a disposition applied room by room.
//   - Hoarding's condition-recognition movement ("is the home getting hard to use?") has no
//     equivalent here. This page has no condition-recognition movement — an estate property is not
//     being diagnosed for a hoarding condition, it is being prepared for its next stage. In its
//     place: property end-goal (sold / rented / returned to family / made usable / emptied), which
//     doc 30 §20A names as this page's own second mechanism.
//   - This page adds two movements with no Hoarding equivalent: remote/out-of-area decision-making
//     and deadlines, both flagged in the brief as "especially relevant to Estate Cleanout" and
//     absent from Hoarding entirely.
//   - The appraisal/estate-sale boundary and the donation/disposal split are estate-specific
//     objections Hoarding does not carry in this shape.
// DISTINCTION FROM PROPERTY CLEANOUTS (`/property-cleanouts-san-jose/`, not rebuilt this pass):
//   that route remains the broader landlord/turnover/general-clearing intent per
//   docs/30-WEBSITE-MASTER-SPEC.md §20A.2's route table. This page does not absorb that scope —
//   the fit section explicitly routes a landlord/turnover situation to Property Cleanouts rather
//   than re-explaining it here.
//
// WHAT THIS REPLACES. The previous build: a 29-line wrapper around the shared
// `ServicePageLayout.astro` template, fed by `estatePage` in doc27ServicePages.ts — the same
// generic 14-page template `/property-cleanouts-san-jose/` still uses. That template's section
// sequence is identical across all 14 pages with only the record's strings changing, which is
// exactly the "one template, swap the noun" failure mode §20A.5 and §20A.25 Q14 rule out.
//
// ROUTE STATE IS UNCHANGED AND DELIBERATELY SO. Same slug, same canonical, same `index, follow`,
// same sitemap membership. The BreadcrumbList trail changes from /property-clearing/ (unchanged
// parent hub) through to this page's own H1. SEO title and meta description are rewritten here —
// see the note beside those constants — because the previous template pulled them from
// `estatePage.seoTitle` / `.metaDescription`, which described "Estate Cleanout in San Jose & South
// Bay" using the OLD lead. New title/description describe the same route and the same service, in
// this page's own words, per §20A.22 ("do not create a second page for a keyword variation" — this
// is one page, one route, refreshed copy).
//
// NO CredentialBar ON THIS ROUTE. Same reasoning as Hoarding: "Insured · COI available on request"
// and "Endorsed · Organic pathogen endorsement" are release-gated by AGENTS.md §3 pending a
// current COI match. Taking a gated claim off an indexable page is the conservative direction.
//
// NO EMBEDDED FORM. /request-assessment/ (this page's former destination, with its
// `?service=estate-cleanout` preselect into AssessmentForm.astro) was retired 2026-09-06 —
// owner decision, docs/05-CURRENT-DECISIONS.md. This page still has no form of its own, so its
// CTAs now follow the standing fallback rule and link to the Contact page's embedded form.
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import BaseLayout from "@layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../../assets/aseptaclean/hoarding-garage-contents.png";
import { getServiceCityLinks } from "@data/serviceCityPages";
import { legal, site } from "@data/site";

// HERO REPOINTED 2026-09-06 to the owner image package, `hoarding-garage-contents`.
//
// It replaces `hero-development-residence-asurnipal.jpg` (Asurnipal, Wikimedia Commons,
// CC BY-SA 4.0), which shipped here as a full-bleed hero with its attribution living only in a
// source comment — never on the rendered page. That is the licence exposure recorded in
// docs/06-ASSET-MANIFEST.md ("STILL SHIPPING — one file, unattributed"), and removing the
// placement is what closes it for this route. The comment also documented that file as "already
// used as the full-bleed hero on `/`" — stale since the 2026-09-05 owner image install repointed
// the homepage hero to `home-hero-neglected-interior`, so the shared-reuse rationale no longer
// held either.
//
// SUBJECT MATCH. This page's mechanism is the Save List: protecting named items — documents,
// photographs, jewelry, collectibles — before an authorized clearance. `hoarding-garage-contents`
// shows belongings staged for sorting, matching "what stays, what goes, what needs review" — the
// same decision the Save List runs before clearing starts. The Commons file it replaces was a
// hoarding-condition living room, which this page's own design note above says it deliberately
// does not run (no condition-recognition movement here).
//
// STILL ORIGINAL ILLUSTRATIVE WORK, not Aseptaclean project documentation, not a client property
// and not proof of a completed job. `[ATMOS]`-class under docs/21-CLAIMS-AND-COMPLIANCE-LAW.md §6:
// no crew, no vehicle, no equipment, no work in progress, no before/after pair. Provenance is
// recorded in docs/06-ASSET-MANIFEST.md and nowhere on the page.
//
// Sibling heroes stay distinct: Property Cleanouts keeps `home-property-scope-detail` and Move-Out
// keeps `property-interior-introduction`, so no two of the three cleanout-family pages open on the
// same photograph. Hoarding's own hero (package slot 03, `hoarding-living-room`) is a different
// file from this one, so the PPC/SEO hoarding pages and this page still do not share an image.

const canonicalPath = "/estate-cleanout-san-jose/";

// REWRITTEN FROM `estatePage.seoTitle` / `.metaDescription` (doc27ServicePages.ts), which
// described the page's OLD lead ("Estate cleanout in San Jose without deciding everything
// today"). This rebuild's H1 and thesis differ from that lead, so the title/description are
// brought into agreement with what the page now actually says, per §20A.22.
const title = "Estate Cleanout in San Jose | Aseptaclean";
const description =
  "Estate cleanout in San Jose for families, executors and trustees. Important items are protected and a save list is agreed before any approved contents leave the property.";

// §20A.8's destination rule + §20A.9's service branch. `?service=estate-cleanout` is read by
// AssessmentForm.astro, added alongside this page (see that component's service-context block).
// It maps to the existing "Inherited or estate property" `property_situation` option — already
// present on the form, not invented for this page — and records the real entry route on the lead.
const assessmentHref = "/contact/#contact-form";
const phoneHref = site.business.phoneUri;
const smsHref = site.business.smsUri;

// AGENTS.md §2's crawl-path note records six service × city routes as deliberately linked from
// this indexable page. `getServiceCityLinks("estate-cleanout")` is an existing, whitelisted call —
// `ServiceSlug` in cityFacts.ts is `"hoarding-cleanup" | "estate-cleanout"` — the same mechanism
// Hoarding's rebuild uses for its own three cities.
const cityLinks = getServiceCityLinks("estate-cleanout");

// §39's "what may be under the contents" list, reworded from the landing-page source's plain
// nouns into the register a family or executor would actually use, and reordered so the
// irreplaceable items lead rather than trail behind clothing and tools.
const contents = [
  "wills, deeds and titles",
  "tax and insurance papers",
  "identification and keys",
  "photographs and letters",
  "jewelry and cash",
  "items named for a specific person",
  "collectibles",
  "clothing and furniture",
  "tools and household items",
  "ordinary contents with no remaining claim on them"
] as const;

// The Save List. §39's examples, kept as the range from a broad standing rule to a single named
// object — that range is the point: a save list can be as short as one sentence.
const saveListRules = [
  { label: "Save all", detail: "family photographs, legal papers, keys, jewelry" },
  { label: "Look for", detail: "a specific named item — “the red photo album,” a ring, a will" },
  { label: "Do not enter", detail: "a room, closet or storage area held out of the scope entirely" },
  { label: "Set aside", detail: "anything with a name on it, for later review" }
] as const;

// §20A the property's end goal. Doc 30 names this page's second mechanism explicitly: the end
// goal changes the sequence, the scope and the deadline.
const goals = [
  { label: "Sold", detail: "cleared so the property can move toward listing and sale" },
  { label: "Rented", detail: "cleared and cleaned ahead of repair and the next tenant" },
  { label: "Returned to family", detail: "named items separated out before the rest is cleared" },
  { label: "Made usable again", detail: "rooms, paths or storage areas cleared for present use" },
  { label: "Emptied", detail: "approved contents removed, property ready for its next step" }
] as const;

// Decision-authority questions. §39's list, kept operational rather than legalistic — Aseptaclean
// establishes a clear authorized contact; it does not determine ownership or legal authority.
const authority = [
  "who can approve the project",
  "who decides what stays and what may leave",
  "who the crew contacts when something is unclear",
  "which areas, if any, are off-limits",
  "whether other family members need access during the work"
] as const;

// §39's six-stage property plan, expanded to include the review/handoff close the source's
// five-step list stops short of — homepage.sampleRecord and /handoff-standard/ both describe a
// closeout step, and a process that stops before handoff would contradict the rest of the site.
const process = [
  {
    title: "Protect important items",
    detail:
      "The save list is applied first — papers, photographs, valuables and any named items are set aside before anything else is touched."
  },
  {
    title: "Confirm keep, review and remove rules",
    detail:
      "Categories are agreed in writing with the authorized decision-maker: what stays, what is held for review, what is approved to leave."
  },
  {
    title: "Clear approved areas, one at a time",
    detail: "Work moves room by room against the written plan, not at random and not by whichever area is nearest the door."
  },
  {
    title: "Handle approved material the way the plan says",
    detail:
      "Where a container is part of the job, it is coordinated in advance — placement, timing and what it can legally hold."
  },
  {
    title: "Clean cleared areas, when included",
    detail: "Once a room is open, surfaces in scope are cleaned. Not automatic — written into the plan or it does not happen."
  },
  {
    title: "Final review",
    detail: "The completed scope is walked through against the plan, with any open items still listed as open."
  }
] as const;

// What clearing can expose. Kept parallel to Hoarding's list — the same conditions can hide under
// contents whether the property is an accumulation or an estate — with the identical rule that
// "mold-like growth" is never shortened to "mold" (doc 21 §2.1).
const discoveries = [
  "rodent droppings",
  "animal waste",
  "water damage",
  "mold-like growth",
  "sharp objects",
  "human biological material",
  "damaged flooring or walls",
  "heavy buildup not visible from the doorway"
] as const;

// Boundaries. Combines doc27ServicePages.ts's `estatePage.boundaries` (owner-reconciled,
// route-audit-corrected) with §39's donation/appraisal detail the data record compresses.
const boundaries = [
  "valuation, appraisal or estate-sale services",
  "legal decisions about ownership or who is entitled to an item",
  "removal of anything without authorized direction",
  "transporting contents off the property",
  "structural repair, drywall, flooring or construction",
  "human biological material, regulated waste or sharps handling",
  "pest inspection, identification, exclusion or treatment",
  "stating that a property is safe, cleared or fit to sell"
] as const;

const goodFit = [
  "a family or representative is managing estate contents",
  "important items are mixed in with things that can go",
  "the property is being prepared for sale, rental or transfer",
  "more than one person is involved and the decisions need one clear process",
  "cleaning is needed once rooms are cleared",
  "you want the scope in writing before work starts",
  "a simple junk pickup would not cover what the property actually needs"
] as const;

const poorFit = [
  "everything in the property is already approved to go and a truck is all this needs",
  "you need an estate-sale company or an appraiser",
  "the main need is construction, repair or renovation",
  "the condition is outside our current scope"
] as const;

const priceDrivers = [
  "size of the property and how many rooms are affected",
  "how much sorting the family wants before removal",
  "amount of contents and how densely a property is filled",
  "access — stairs, parking, carry distance",
  "crew size and number of days",
  "cleaning after clearing, when it is included",
  "disposal and container needs",
  "conditions that were not visible at the start",
  "your deadline"
] as const;

// §20A.18: 4–7 highest-value, service-specific questions. Answers reconciled from
// doc27ServicePages.ts's `estatePage.faq` (owner-approved: "No" to appraisal, "Yes" to
// post-clearing cleaning) plus §39's stronger objection set — remote/out-of-state, found money or
// jewelry, donation acceptance — which the data record's two-question set does not cover.
const faq = [
  [
    "Do you buy or appraise estate items?",
    [
      "No. Appraisal, resale and estate-sale services are separate from what Aseptaclean does.",
      "If the family believes something may have value, have it reviewed before it is approved for removal — we follow the sorting and removal plan you set, not a valuation we make ourselves."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "What happens if you find money, jewelry or documents we did not know about?",
    [
      "The crew stops, sets it aside, and follows the job's contact process.",
      "We do not make fast decisions about someone else's belongings. If it is on the save list or looks like it should be, it waits for you."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "Do we need to sort everything before you arrive?",
    [
      "No. But the more that is already decided — known keeps, items approved to leave, who can make the calls — the faster the clearing can move.",
      "Tell us what you are looking for before work starts and named items go into the plan."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "Can family members take items while the crew is working?",
    [
      "This needs to be planned in advance. People arriving to take things while a crew is mid-clearing can cause real confusion about what was already approved to leave.",
      "Tell us before the job if family members still need access, and we will build it into the plan."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "Can items be donated?",
    [
      "Donation can be part of the plan, but acceptance is controlled by the donation center — not by us.",
      "Not everything is accepted; condition, type, size and the center's current needs all matter. Donation items stay separate from disposal items so nothing gets mixed up on site."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "Do you clean the property after it is cleared?",
    [
      "Yes, when cleaning is written into the scope.",
      "How much a property needs usually cannot be judged until the area is open, which is why clearing and cleaning are scoped as separate phases rather than one assumed package."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "What if the family is not in California?",
    [
      "Tell us. Depending on the property and who holds authority to decide, the project can often run on photographs, a written scope and a clear remote contact person.",
      "The clearer the rules are before work starts — save list, off-limit areas, who we call with a question — the easier that becomes."
    ]
  ]
] as const;

// Service + BreadcrumbList + FAQPage. Breadcrumb position 2 stays "Complex Property Cleanup" /
// /property-clearing/, matching the megaNav grouping and Hoarding's own breadcrumb parent.
// FAQPage regenerated from the seven visible questions — never padded beyond what renders
// (doc 21 §6.1).
const schema = [
  {
    "@type": "Service",
    name: "Estate Cleanout in San Jose",
    serviceType: "Estate cleanout",
    provider: { "@id": new URL("/#business", site.urls.site).href },
    areaServed: { "@type": "City", name: "San Jose" }
  },
  {
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: site.urls.home },
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: 2,
        name: "Complex Property Cleanup",
        item: new URL("/property-clearing/", site.urls.site).href
      },
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: 3,
        name: "Estate Cleanouts",
        item: new URL(canonicalPath, site.urls.site).href
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: faq.map(([question, answer]) => ({
      "@type": "Question",
      name: question,
      acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: answer.join("\n\n") }
    }))
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath={canonicalPath} schema={schema}>
  {/* ══ 01 · SERVICE HERO ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      §20A.7: location context → unmistakable service H1 → specialist thesis → service CTA →
      phone as secondary → service-relevant image. */}
  <section class="ec-hero" aria-labelledby="estate-title">
    <div class="ec-hero__media">
      <Image
        src={heroImage}
        alt=""
        layout="full-width"
        widths={[480, 768, 1024, 1440, 1920]}
        sizes="100vw"
        format="webp"
        quality={74}
        loading="eager"
        decoding="async"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>

    <div class="ec-hero__overlay" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <div class="ec-hero__shell">
      <div class="ec-hero__content">
        <p class="ec-hero__eyebrow">
          Estate cleanout · San Jose · {site.location.serviceArea}
        </p>
        <h1 id="estate-title" class="ac-type-h1-hub">Estate Cleanout</h1>
        <p class="ec-hero__lede">
          A full home can hold years of papers, photos, furniture and family items. We build the
          plan before anything is approved to leave the property.
        </p>
        {/* §20A.5's specialist thesis. This is the sentence the whole page exists to make
            credible — the brief names it as the core service argument. */}
        <p class="ec-hero__thesis">Protect what matters before the property gets cleared.</p>

        <div class="ec-hero__actions">
          <a class="ec-btn ec-btn--light" href={assessmentHref}>{site.offer.secondaryCta}</a>
          {phoneHref && (
            <a class="ec-hero__call" href={phoneHref}>Call {site.business.phone}</a>
          )}
        </div>

        <p class="ec-hero__reassurance">
          We do not start by throwing things away. We start with what cannot be replaced.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 02 · PROTECT WHAT MATTERS ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      The page's signature movement and its only full navy field — mirroring Hoarding's placement
      (a navy field as movement 02–03) without mirroring its content. Where Hoarding's navy
      movement is a five-category register applied to accumulated contents, this one is a save
      list: named instructions an authorized person writes before clearing starts. */}
  <section class="ec-save" aria-labelledby="save-title">
    <div class="ec-save__inner">
      <div class="ec-save__lead">
        <p class="ec-save__label">What we are protecting first</p>
        <h2 id="save-title" class="ac-type-h2-home-feature">
          We do not start by clearing.
        </h2>
        <p class="ec-save__lede">
          Before anything leaves the property, we look for what cannot be replaced. Once something
          is gone, getting it back may not be possible — so the plan starts with protecting it, not
          with the truck.
        </p>
        <p>A full property usually holds a mix of:</p>
        <ul class="ec-inline-list">
          {contents.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p class="ec-statement">
          A full house is not one big pile to be cleared. It is a house with things in it that
          still matter to someone.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ec-savelist">
        <div class="ec-savelist__head">
          <p class="ec-savelist__title">Save List</p>
          <p class="ec-savelist__flag">Sample · not a client record</p>
        </div>
        <p class="ec-savelist__intro">
          Tell us what to watch for before work starts. Instructions can be as broad or as specific
          as the family needs:
        </p>
        <dl class="ec-savelist__rules">
          {saveListRules.map((rule) => (
            <div class="ec-savelist__row">
              <dt>{rule.label}</dt>
              <dd>{rule.detail}</dd>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>
        <p class="ec-savelist__note">
          Those rules go into the written plan. We cannot promise that every missing item will be
          found — but the crew knows what matters before the clearing starts, not after.
        </p>
        <p class="ec-savelist__disclaimer">{legal.documentationDisclaimer}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 03 · WHO DECIDES + WHAT THE PROPERTY NEEDS TO BECOME ════════════════════════════════
      Doc 30 §20A's two required movements for this page: decision authority, then the property's
      end goal. Kept as one section because in practice they are asked together — who can approve
      this, and what are we approving it toward. */}
  <section class="ec-decide" aria-labelledby="decide-title">
    <div class="ec-decide__inner">
      <div class="ec-decide__copy">
        <p class="ec-label">The decision process</p>
        <h2 id="decide-title" class="ac-type-h2-home-feature">
          One property needs one clear decision process.
        </h2>
        <p class="ec-lede">
          Estate and family jobs get complicated when one person says keep it, another says
          remove it, and a third says nobody should touch it yet. Before work starts, we need to
          know:
        </p>
        <ul class="ec-check-list">
          {authority.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p class="ec-decide__note">
          Aseptaclean does not determine who owns a property or who is legally entitled to an
          item. We need one authorized contact and one clear process — the family, executor or
          trustee establishes both.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ec-goals">
        <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">What does the property need to become?</h3>
        <p>
          The end goal changes the sequence, the scope and the deadline. We need to know where the
          property is going before we can plan how to get it there.
        </p>
        <dl class="ec-goals__list">
          {goals.map((goal) => (
            <div class="ec-goals__row">
              <dt>{goal.label}</dt>
              <dd>{goal.detail}</dd>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 04 · THE CLEARING PLAN ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      §20A.14: vertical numbered sequence, not six equal cards. Stop-and-ask and hidden conditions
      close it, because both are things that happen DURING the sequence rather than steps in it. */}
  <section class="ec-process" aria-labelledby="process-title">
    <div class="ec-process__inner">
      <div class="ec-process__head">
        <p class="ec-label">The clearing plan</p>
        <h2 id="process-title" class="ac-type-h2-home-feature">We do not start at random.</h2>
        <p class="ec-lede">
          Protect first, then decide, then clear — in that order, not the reverse. A large
          property can be broken into parts when that is the sensible way to run the job.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ol class="ec-steps">
        {process.map((step, index) => (
          <li class="ec-step">
            <span class="ec-step__index" aria-hidden="true">
              {String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}
            </span>
            <div class="ec-step__body">
              <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">{step.title}</h3>
              <p>{step.detail}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>

      <div class="ec-process__pair">
        <div class="ec-note">
          <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">If we do not know, we ask.</h3>
          <p>
            The crew should not be making family decisions. Money, jewelry, legal papers, family
            photographs, keys, IDs — anything on the save list or anything that may have value —
            gets stopped, set aside and reported rather than moved.
          </p>
          <p>
            The goal is not to make fast choices with someone else's things. It is to keep the job
            moving without making careless ones.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="ec-note ec-note--stop">
          <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">New condition. New decision.</h3>
          <p>
            Full properties can hide things. Once rooms begin to clear, we may find:
          </p>
          <ul class="ec-inline-list">
            {discoveries.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
          </ul>
          <p>
            If something changes the scope, we stop in that area and tell you before going any
            further. We do not quietly turn it into a bigger bill.
          </p>
          <p class="ec-clause">
            Cleaning only — not a decontamination, sterilization, or health-safety determination.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="ec-cleaning">
        <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">A cleared house is not always a clean house.</h3>
        <p>
          A bedroom full of furniture, boxes and papers cannot be fully cleaned yet — the floor,
          baseboards, shelves and corners underneath are not visible from the doorway. Once
          approved contents are addressed, cleaning in scope can cover floors, baseboards,
          shelves, cabinets, counters, kitchens, bathrooms, closets and other reachable surfaces.
          It is included when it is written into the plan. It is not automatic.
        </p>
        <a class="ec-link" href="/deep-cleaning-san-jose/">
          <span>Detailed deep cleaning</span> <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 05 · CONTENTS, DISPOSAL, DONATION, BOUNDARIES ════════════════════════════════════════
      §20A.13: boundaries are conversion content, not something to hide. This is the estate-
      specific objection set Hoarding does not carry in this shape — appraisal, donation
      acceptance, who actually hauls material off the property. */}
  <section class="ec-boundaries" aria-labelledby="boundaries-title">
    <div class="ec-boundaries__inner">
      <div class="ec-boundaries__head">
        <p class="ec-label">What happens to the contents</p>
        <h2 id="boundaries-title" class="ac-type-h2-home-feature">
          Removal is part of the plan, not a guess on the day.
        </h2>
        <p class="ec-lede">
          Aseptaclean is not a hauling company. We sort, bag, stage, carry out and load approved
          material; disposal and transport off the property are handled by a City-authorized
          hauler engaged for the project. Where a container is part of the job, we coordinate its
          placement in advance.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ec-boundaries__pair">
        <div class="ec-donation">
          <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">If some items can be donated</h3>
          <p>
            Tell us before the job. Donation items stay separate from disposal items so nothing
            gets mixed up on site.
          </p>
          <p>
            Not everything can be donated. A donation center may decline an item based on
            condition, type, size, or its current needs — we cannot promise every item will be
            accepted, but if donation is part of the plan we need to know before clearing begins.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="ec-appraisal">
          <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">If items may have value</h3>
          <p>
            Aseptaclean is not an estate-sale company or an appraiser. If the family believes
            something may be worth having reviewed, have it evaluated before approving it for
            removal — that boundary protects the estate, not just us.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="ec-boundaries__list">
        <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">What an estate cleanout does not include</h3>
        <ul class="ec-minus-list">
          {boundaries.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p class="ec-boundaries__clause">
          Aseptaclean may clean accepted conditions left behind after an appropriately licensed
          pest operator has confirmed the active pest issue is resolved. Aseptaclean does not
          inspect for, identify, exclude, trap, or treat pests.
        </p>
        <p class="ec-disclaimer">{legal.scopeDisclaimer}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 06 · WHAT PROOF EXISTS + REMOTE SUPPORT + FIT + PRICE ═══════════════════════════════
      Combines §20A.15 (the honest evidence gap), the remote/out-of-area movement the brief flags
      as especially relevant to this page, deadlines, and fit/price logic. Five jobs, one movement
      — §20A.6 licenses combining jobs where that produces stronger rhythm rather than a page of
      eleven bands. */}
  <section class="ec-fit" aria-labelledby="fit-title">
    <div class="ec-fit__inner">
      <div class="ec-fit__lead">
        <p class="ec-label">Evidence, remote support, and fit</p>
        <h2 id="fit-title" class="ac-type-h2-home-feature">
          You do not have to be at the property every day.
        </h2>
        <p class="ec-lede">
          There is no photo gallery on this page. Aseptaclean has no photographed estate project
          to show yet, and a set of stock before-and-after images would tell you nothing true
          about how this company works. What we can offer instead is a clear plan a family does
          not have to be present to run.
        </p>
        <p>
          Some families are dealing with a Bay Area property while living somewhere else. If the
          project allows it, we can work from an authorized contact, a written scope, a save list
          and agreed off-limit areas — set before work starts rather than negotiated on site.
          The clearer those rules are in advance, the easier remote coordination becomes. We do
          not promise a remote capability beyond what the project and the authorized contact can
          actually support.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ec-fit__columns">
        <div class="ec-fit__col">
          <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">Usually a fit</h3>
          <ul class="ec-check-list">
            {goodFit.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
          </ul>
          <p class="ec-fit__aside">
            If the situation is a landlord or property-manager turnover rather than a family or
            estate matter, <a href="/property-cleanouts-san-jose/">property cleanouts</a> is
            usually the closer match. If the property's main issue is heavy accumulation rather
            than an estate handoff, <a href="/hoarding-cleanup-san-jose/">hoarding cleanup</a>
            covers that.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="ec-fit__col ec-fit__col--out">
          <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">Probably not a fit</h3>
          <ul class="ec-minus-list">
            {poorFit.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
          </ul>
          <p class="ec-fit__aside">
            If a junk hauler is genuinely all the property needs, use one. We do not need to turn
            a small pickup into a large project.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="ec-deadline">
        <p class="ec-price__label">If there is a deadline</p>
        <p>
          A sale date, a waiting buyer, a lease ending, or a dumpster with a fixed window can all
          change crew size, work order, disposal plan, schedule and price. Tell us as early as
          possible — not on the last day the home has to be empty.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ec-price">
        <div class="ec-price__copy">
          <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-feature">The price depends on the property.</h3>
          <p>
            A one-bedroom condo and a four-bedroom house with a packed garage are not the same
            job, and a published starting figure would only ever be honest about one of them.
            Larger cleanouts usually need photographs and a walkthrough before a firm scope and
            price.
          </p>
          <p class="ec-price__fee">{site.offer.assessmentFraming(site.offer.assessmentFee)}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ec-price__drivers">
          <p class="ec-price__label">What moves the number</p>
          <ul class="ec-rule-list ec-rule-list--tight">
            {priceDrivers.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      {cityLinks.length > 0 && (
        <div class="ec-local">
          <p class="ec-price__label">Local planning notes</p>
          <p>
            Container placement, permit review times and street access are set by the city the
            property sits in. These sourced guides do not confirm current service availability —
            share the address so Aseptaclean can confirm the project is in range.
          </p>
          <ul class="ec-local__links">
            {cityLinks.map((link) => (
              <li>
                <a href={link.href}>{link.anchor} <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 07 · QUESTIONS ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      Open Q&A, not an accordion — same reasoning as Hoarding: a nervous buyer should not need to
      click to find out whether Aseptaclean appraises jewelry. */}
  <section class="ec-faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
    <div class="ec-faq__inner">
      <div class="ec-faq__head">
        <p class="ec-label">Questions</p>
        <h2 id="faq-title" class="ac-type-h2-home-feature">The questions that decide this.</h2>
        <p class="ec-lede">
          Seven that come up on nearly every estate project. The general ones — scheduling,
          payment, what happens on the day — are on the <a href="/faq/">main FAQ</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ec-faq__grid">
        {faq.map(([question, answer]) => (
          <article class="ec-qa">
            <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">{question}</h3>
            {answer.map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 08 · CLOSE ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      §39's approved closing direction — start with what needs to stay, not how fast things can be
      thrown away. Typographic on navy, not image-led: the hero already carries the one photograph
      this page uses. */}
  <section class="ec-close" aria-labelledby="close-title">
    <div class="ec-close__inner">
      <div class="ec-close__copy">
        <h2 id="close-title" class="ac-type-h2-final">
          You do not have to decide everything today.
        </h2>
        <p class="ec-close__lede">
          Start with what needs to stay, what can leave, and what the property needs to become.
          Then we can build the job.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ec-close__action">
        <a class="ec-btn ec-btn--light" href={assessmentHref}>{site.offer.secondaryCta}</a>
        <div class="ec-close__secondary">
          {phoneHref && <a href={phoneHref}>Call {site.business.phone}</a>}
          {smsHref && <a href={smsHref}>{site.offer.smsCta}</a>}
        </div>
        <p class="ec-close__note">
          Submitting a request does not authorize work or create a service agreement.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Shared page primitives — the same system Hoarding's rebuild established (spacing, rule and
     list treatment), reused under an `.ec-` prefix so the two pages' styles never collide even
     though both ship as single scoped <style> blocks. NOTHING in this file sets font-size on a
     heading tag or on a selector that resolves to one; heading sizes come from `.ac-type-*` role
     classes and are tuned by overriding the role class's TOKEN on a container (AGENTS.md §6 law 1).
     ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

  .ec-label {
    max-width: none;
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    color: var(--ac-color-blue-deep);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.11em;
    line-height: 1.4;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .ec-lede {
    margin: 22px 0 0;
    max-width: 54ch;
    color: var(--ac-color-ink-600);
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .ec-statement {
    margin: 24px 0 0;
    max-width: 46ch;
    color: var(--ac-color-navy-900);
    font-size: 1.1875rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.45;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .ec-inline-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    max-width: none;
    margin: 16px 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    gap: 4px 10px;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .ec-inline-list li {
    max-width: none;
    color: var(--ac-color-ink-600);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .ec-inline-list li:not(:last-child)::after {
    margin-left: 10px;
    color: var(--ac-color-line-strong);
    content: "·";
  }

  .ec-rule-list {
    display: grid;
    max-width: none;
    margin: 20px 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    gap: 0 44px;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    list-style: none;
  }

  .ec-rule-list li {
    max-width: none;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--ac-color-line);
    padding: 12px 0;
    color: var(--ac-color-ink-600);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .ec-rule-list--tight li {
    padding: 10px 0;
  }

  .ec-check-list,
  .ec-minus-list {
    max-width: none;
    margin: 18px 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .ec-check-list li,
  .ec-minus-list li {
    position: relative;
    max-width: none;
    padding: 9px 0 9px 26px;
    color: var(--ac-color-ink-600);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .ec-check-list li + li,
  .ec-minus-list li + li {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--ac-color-line);
  }

  /* CSS marks, not inline SVG — see Hoarding's identical note: these are rules, not icons, and
     a decorative glyph per row is noise a screen reader has to be told to ignore. */
  .ec-check-list li::before,
  .ec-minus-list li::before {
    position: absolute;
    top: 17px;
    left: 0;
    width: 12px;
    height: 2px;
    background: var(--ac-color-blue-deep);
    content: "";
  }

  .ec-minus-list li::before {
    background: var(--ac-color-exclusion-red);
  }

  .ec-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    min-height: 44px;
    align-items: center;
    margin-top: 18px;
    gap: 0.4em;
    color: var(--ac-color-navy-800);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-decoration: none;
    text-underline-offset: 4px;
  }

  .ec-link:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .ec-btn {
    display: inline-flex;
    min-height: 52px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    border-radius: 2px;
    padding-inline: 30px;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    font-weight: 750;
    line-height: 1;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: background-color 150ms ease;
  }

  .ec-btn--light {
    background: var(--ac-color-paper);
    color: var(--ac-color-navy-900);
  }

  .ec-btn--light:hover {
    background: var(--ac-color-blue-pale);
  }

  .ec-btn--light:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--ac-color-paper);
    outline-offset: 4px;
  }

  .ec-clause,
  .ec-boundaries__clause {
    margin: 18px 0 0;
    max-width: 60ch;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--ac-color-navy-800);
    padding: 2px 0 2px 16px;
    color: var(--ac-color-navy-900);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .ec-disclaimer {
    margin: 24px 0 0;
    max-width: 78ch;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--ac-color-line);
    padding-top: 18px;
    color: var(--ac-color-ink-600);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  /* ══ 01 · HERO ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

  .ec-hero {
    --ac-text-h1-hub: 2.875rem;
    position: relative;
    min-height: 540px;
    overflow: hidden;
    padding: 0;
    background: var(--ac-color-navy-950);
  }

  .ec-hero__media,
  .ec-hero__overlay {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }

  .ec-hero__media :global(img) {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: 62% 55%;
  }

  /* Flat overlay, doc 30 §20A.7 — no gradient, no vignette. Matches Hoarding's measured 0.64
     value: this photograph carries similarly bright mid-tones and the copy column runs a
     comparable number of elements deep, so the same opacity is the safe starting point.
     Re-run scripts/hoarding-hero-contrast.mjs (or an equivalent pass) against this route's own
     crop before launch if the value below has not been independently verified here. */
  .ec-hero__overlay {
    z-index: 1;
    background: rgb(10 30 45 / 64%);
  }

  .ec-hero__shell {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;
    display: flex;
    width: min(100% - 64px, 1320px);
    min-height: inherit;
    align-items: center;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-block: 72px;
  }

  .ec-hero__content {
    max-width: 620px;
    color: var(--ac-color-paper);
  }

  .ec-hero__eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 18px;
    max-width: none;
    color: rgb(255 255 255 / 92%);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.11em;
    line-height: 1.4;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .ec-hero__content h1 {
    max-width: 12ch;
    color: var(--ac-color-paper);
  }

  .ec-hero__lede {
    margin: 20px 0 0;
    max-width: 46ch;
    color: #f1f5fa;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.62;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .ec-hero__thesis {
    margin: 14px 0 0;
    max-width: 36ch;
    color: var(--ac-color-paper);
    font-size: 1.1875rem;
    font-weight: 650;
    line-height: 1.4;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .ec-hero__actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    margin-top: 30px;
    gap: 8px 26px;
  }

  .ec-hero__call {
    display: inline-flex;
    min-height: 44px;
    align-items: center;
    color: var(--ac-color-paper);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-underline-offset: 5px;
  }

  .ec-hero__call:hover {
    color: var(--ac-color-blue-pale);
  }

  .ec-hero__call:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--ac-color-paper);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }

  .ec-hero__reassurance {
    margin: 22px 0 0;
    max-width: 46ch;
    color: rgb(255 255 255 / 95%);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  /* ══ 02 · PROTECT WHAT MATTERS / SAVE LIST ═════════════════════════════════════════════ */

  .ec-save {
    background: var(--ac-color-navy-900);
    padding-block: clamp(80px, 8vw, 120px);
  }

  .ec-save__inner {
    --ac-text-h2-home-feature: 2.5rem;
    display: grid;
    width: min(100% - 64px, 1320px);
    align-items: start;
    margin-inline: auto;
    gap: 52px 80px;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.05fr);
  }

  .ec-save__label {
    max-width: none;
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    color: var(--ac-color-blue-on-navy);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.11em;
    line-height: 1.4;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .ec-save__lead h2 {
    max-width: 15ch;
    color: var(--ac-color-paper);
  }

  .ec-save__lede {
    margin: 24px 0 0;
    max-width: 46ch;
    color: #ccd9e8;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.62;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .ec-save__lead > p:not(.ec-save__label):not(.ec-save__lede):not(.ec-statement) {
    margin: 18px 0 0;
    max-width: 46ch;
    color: #a9bdd4;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .ec-save__lead .ec-inline-list li {
    color: #ccd9e8;
  }

  .ec-save__lead .ec-inline-list li:not(:last-child)::after {
    color: rgb(255 255 255 / 30%);
  }

  .ec-save__lead .ec-statement {
    color: var(--ac-color-paper);
    max-width: 44ch;
  }

  /* The Save List document — drawn as a physical artifact on the navy field, echoing Hoarding's
     .hc-register document treatment but with its own content shape (rule → instruction, not
     category → definition) and its own title. */
  .ec-savelist {
    border-top: 3px solid var(--ac-color-blue-on-navy);
    background: rgb(255 255 255 / 6%);
    padding: 26px 28px 28px;
  }

  .ec-savelist__head {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 6px 18px;
  }

  .ec-savelist__title {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: none;
    color: var(--ac-color-paper);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    font-weight: 750;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .ec-savelist__flag {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: none;
    color: #e3c675;
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 750;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .ec-savelist__intro {
    margin: 16px 0 0;
    max-width: none;
    color: #ccd9e8;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .ec-savelist__rules {
    margin: 18px 0 0;
  }

  .ec-savelist__row {
    display: grid;
    align-items: baseline;
    border-top: 1px solid rgb(255 255 255 / 16%);
    padding: 16px 0;
    gap: 4px 24px;
